### Why does the default time zone matter?

A Java application's "default time zone" is not a single switch. It is resolved from several layers, and inconsistency between them causes the classic "works on my machine, wrong in production" time bug.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import java.util.TimeZone;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class TimeZoneProblem {

    private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(
        TimeZoneProblem.class.getName());

    public static void demonstrate() {
        // Developer's machine: IST (Asia/Kolkata, UTC+5:30)
        // Production server: UTC
        // Result: timestamps differ by 5.5 hours

        LOG.info("JVM timezone: " + TimeZone.getDefault().getID());
        LOG.info("Current time: " + new java.util.Date());
        // On dev:  2024-06-15 14:30:00 IST
        // On prod: 2024-06-15 09:00:00 UTC
        // Same instant, different representation
    }
}
```

### How do you set the JVM time zone?

The preferred method is the system property `-Duser.timezone=UTC`. Alternatively, call `TimeZone.setDefault()` very early in startup. This affects every `new Date()`, legacy `Calendar`, and any code using the system default.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import java.util.TimeZone;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class TimeZoneConfig {

    private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(
        TimeZoneConfig.class.getName());

    /**
     * Call this ONCE at application startup, before any date processing.
     */
    public static void initializeUtc() {
        TimeZone.setDefault(TimeZone.getTimeZone("UTC"));
        LOG.info("JVM timezone set to: " + TimeZone.getDefault().getID());
    }
}
```

```text
# Preferred: JVM argument
java -Duser.timezone=UTC -jar bank-service.jar

# Dockerfile
ENV TZ=UTC
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-Duser.timezone=UTC", "-jar", "bank-service.jar"]
```

### How do you set the Jackson serialization time zone?

Spring Boot's `spring.jackson.time-zone` property tells Jackson which zone to use when serializing and deserializing date fields.

```text
# application.properties
spring.jackson.time-zone=UTC
spring.jackson.date-format=yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss.SSSZ
```

```java
package com.bank.config;

import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.SerializationFeature;
import java.util.TimeZone;

public class JacksonConfig {

    public ObjectMapper objectMapper() {
        ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
        mapper.setTimeZone(TimeZone.getTimeZone("UTC"));
        mapper.disable(SerializationFeature.WRITE_DATES_AS_TIMESTAMPS);
        return mapper;
    }
}
```

### How do you set the database session time zone?

Align the JDBC or Hibernate time zone so the driver interprets timestamps consistently. Without this, writing a `TIMESTAMP` to the database can silently shift by the server's offset.

```text
# Hibernate property
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.jdbc.time_zone=UTC

# JDBC URL parameter (PostgreSQL)
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:postgresql://host:5432/bankdb?options=-c timezone=UTC
```

```sql
-- PostgreSQL: set session timezone
SET timezone = 'UTC';
SHOW timezone;  -- UTC

-- Verify: a timestamp is stored and read in UTC
SELECT NOW() AT TIME ZONE 'UTC' AS utc_now,
       NOW() AT TIME ZONE 'Asia/Kolkata' AS ist_now;
```

### How do you set the Docker/OS time zone?

The Docker `TZ` environment variable sets the container's timezone. The JVM reads this at startup if `-Duser.timezone` is not set.

```text
# Dockerfile
FROM eclipse-temurin:21-jre
ENV TZ=UTC
RUN ln -snf /usr/share/zoneinfo/$TZ /etc/localtime && echo $TZ > /etc/timezone
COPY bank-service.jar /app/bank-service.jar
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-Duser.timezone=UTC", "-jar", "/app/bank-service.jar"]
```

### What happens when the layers disagree?

If the JVM is UTC but the database session is IST, timestamps are silently shifted. This is the source of most timezone bugs.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.sql.*;
import java.time.*;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class TimeZoneMismatchDemo {

    private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(
        TimeZoneMismatchDemo.class.getName());

    public void demonstrateMismatch(Connection conn) throws SQLException {
        // JVM: UTC, Database session: IST (UTC+5:30)
        // Writing: app sends 2024-06-15T09:00:00 UTC
        // DB interprets as: 2024-06-15T09:00:00 IST = 2024-06-15T03:30:00 UTC
        // Reading back: 2024-06-15T03:30:00 UTC
        // Shifted by 5.5 hours!

        LOG.severe("Timezone mismatch: JVM=UTC, DB=IST -> 5.5 hour shift");
        LOG.info("Fix: set hibernate.jdbc.time_zone=UTC");
    }
}
```

### How should you handle user-facing time display?

Store everything in UTC. Convert to the user's local zone only at the presentation edge using explicit `ZonedDateTime`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.time.*;
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;

public class TimeDisplayService {

    private static final DateTimeFormatter DISPLAY_FORMAT =
        DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd-MMM-yyyy hh:mm a z");

    /**
     * Convert a UTC instant to the user's local zone for display.
     */
    public String formatForUser(Instant utcInstant, String userZoneId) {
        ZonedDateTime userTime = utcInstant.atZone(ZoneId.of(userZoneId));
        return userTime.format(DISPLAY_FORMAT);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TimeDisplayService service = new TimeDisplayService();
        Instant now = Instant.parse("2024-06-15T09:00:00Z");

        // Rajesh in Delhi sees IST
        System.out.println(service.formatForUser(now, "Asia/Kolkata"));
        // 15-Jun-2024 02:30 PM IST

        // Priya in London sees BST
        System.out.println(service.formatForUser(now, "Europe/London"));
        // 15-Jun-2024 10:00 AM BST
    }
}
```

```text
Output:
15-Jun-2024 02:30 PM IST
15-Jun-2024 10:00 AM BST
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is setting the timezone at only one layer and assuming the others follow. Another mistake is calling `TimeZone.setDefault()` at runtime, which affects all threads.

```text
Common Mistakes:

  Mistake                           Fix
  ────────────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────
  Set JVM only, forget DB session   Set hibernate.jdbc.time_zone
  Set Docker TZ, forget JVM prop    Always pass -Duser.timezone
  Call setDefault() at runtime      Call once at startup only
  Use local zone in business logic  Store UTC, convert at display
  Rely on host's implicit zone      Set explicitly at every layer
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

`TimeZone.setDefault()` affects the entire JVM process and every thread. Modern code should prefer explicit `java.time` zones over leaning on any default. The near-universal best practice is UTC at every layer.

```text
Senior Checklist:
  [ ] -Duser.timezone=UTC in JVM args
  [ ] spring.jackson.time-zone=UTC in application.properties
  [ ] hibernate.jdbc.time_zone=UTC in JPA properties
  [ ] ENV TZ=UTC in Dockerfile
  [ ] Database session timezone = UTC
  [ ] Business logic uses Instant/ZonedDateTime, never Date()
  [ ] User-facing display converts to local zone at the edge
```
